{"id":"refine_20260928003320784","summary":"Added a global memory for the validated parallel-diagonal ARC filling rule.","rationale":"The rule matched both demonstration pairs with different source colors and correctly solved the test, so it is a reusable, evidence-backed task-family lesson.","expectedOutcome":"For future grids with this same parallel-diagonal structure, identify maximal diagonal segments, fill background cells between consecutive active segments along each r−c value, and preserve the original strokes; validate that the structural pattern applies before using the rule.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_fill_between_parallel_diagonal_stroke_segments","title":"ARC: Fill between parallel diagonal stroke segments","content":"For ARC grids with background 8 and sparse same-color diagonal strokes on lines r+c=s, apply this rule only when that parallel-diagonal structure is present. Split each line into maximal contiguous segments. Represent each segment by its s value and its endpoint interval in v=r-c. For each integer v, find the segments whose intervals contain v, sort them by s, and fill background-8 cells strictly between each consecutive pair with color 2. Preserve all input strokes. This rule matched demonstrations with source colors 4 and 6 and solved a test with source color 7.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"This compact, reusable rule was validated against two demonstrations and a correct solution on a recurring ARC task family.","after":{"id":"arc_fill_between_parallel_diagonal_stroke_segments","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: Fill between parallel diagonal stroke segments","content":"For ARC grids with background 8 and sparse same-color diagonal strokes on lines r+c=s, apply this rule only when that parallel-diagonal structure is present. Split each line into maximal contiguous segments. Represent each segment by its s value and its endpoint interval in v=r-c. For each integer v, find the segments whose intervals contain v, sort them by s, and fill background-8 cells strictly between each consecutive pair with color 2. Preserve all input strokes. This rule matched demonstrations with source colors 4 and 6 and solved a test with source color 7.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T00:33:23.862Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T00:33:23.862Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545240/attempts/f30eea74d21e478487daf9feda584c5e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260928030317412","summary":"Added a focused global ARC memory for the solved color-1/color-6 boundary task, including the distinct divider case.","rationale":"The user explicitly requested a reusable global memory, and the solved test confirms the non-separating rule. The demos also show a separate full-height-divider case, so the memory keeps that exception distinct and emphasizes narrow applicability.","expectedOutcome":"For similar ARC grids, the agent can recognize this pattern, preserve the original 1 and 6 cells, apply the 7 coloring only when the demonstrated structure fits, and check separately for the divider exception.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_color1_color6_boundary_marking","title":"ARC: Color-1/color-6 boundary marking with color 7","content":"For ARC grids matching the demonstrated setup—background 8, color-1 shapes, and a color-6 marker at the upper-right, with output color 7—first check whether the color-1 structure forms a full-height divider. In the non-separating cases (demos 2 and 3 and the solved test), preserve all 1 and 6 cells; recolor to 7 the accessible outer-frame 8 cells and the one-cell 8-neighbor exterior around color-1 components connected to the grid boundary. Leave unrelated interior 8 cells and isolated interior 1 objects unchanged. Distinct divider exception: when a full-height 1 divider separates the 6-side region, keep the divider, clear objects on the opposite side as shown in the demo, and fill the 6-side region with 7. Treat this as a narrow pattern-specific rule; verify the structure against examples before applying, and do not generalize it to unrelated grids.","metadata":{"scope":"global","domain":"ARC","evidence":"User-reported solved test and three demonstrations"},"reason":"Captures the explicitly requested, validated task rule and keeps its demonstrated divider exception separate from the non-separating cases.","after":{"id":"arc_color1_color6_boundary_marking","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: Color-1/color-6 boundary marking with color 7","content":"For ARC grids matching the demonstrated setup—background 8, color-1 shapes, and a color-6 marker at the upper-right, with output color 7—first check whether the color-1 structure forms a full-height divider. In the non-separating cases (demos 2 and 3 and the solved test), preserve all 1 and 6 cells; recolor to 7 the accessible outer-frame 8 cells and the one-cell 8-neighbor exterior around color-1 components connected to the grid boundary. Leave unrelated interior 8 cells and isolated interior 1 objects unchanged. Distinct divider exception: when a full-height 1 divider separates the 6-side region, keep the divider, clear objects on the opposite side as shown in the demo, and fill the 6-side region with 7. Treat this as a narrow pattern-specific rule; verify the structure against examples before applying, and do not generalize it to unrelated grids.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global","domain":"ARC","evidence":"User-reported solved test and three demonstrations"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T03:03:22.247Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T03:03:22.247Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545240/attempts/f30eea74d21e478487daf9feda584c5e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260928040822091","summary":"Created a focused global ARC memory for the validated component-to-band pattern.","rationale":"One demonstration and a correct test submission support this specific rule; it should be applied only when the described workspace and band structure match.","expectedOutcome":"For future matching grids, transfer each top 4-connected component into its color-matched band with the demonstrated alignment and recoloring, while preserving unrelated cells.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_components_into_matching_bands","title":"ARC: Transfer components into matching horizontal bands","content":"For ARC grids matching the demonstrated pattern—a uniform-background top workspace containing small colored components and lower horizontal bands with distinct fill colors and side-marker colors—match each top 4-connected component to the band whose fill color equals the component's color. Clear the source components to the top background. Copy each component's exact mask at the same columns into its matching band, recolor it to that band's side-marker color, and shift it vertically so its lowest cell lands on the band's bottom row. Keep bands, dividers, and unmatched cells unchanged. This rule was validated on one demonstration and a correct test submission; verify the structural match before applying it, rather than treating it as a universal ARC rule.","metadata":{"scope":"global","evidence":"One demonstration pair and one correct test submission"},"reason":"Preserves the explicitly requested reusable, evidence-backed rule with a clear prerequisite against overgeneralization.","after":{"id":"arc_components_into_matching_bands","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: Transfer components into matching horizontal bands","content":"For ARC grids matching the demonstrated pattern—a uniform-background top workspace containing small colored components and lower horizontal bands with distinct fill colors and side-marker colors—match each top 4-connected component to the band whose fill color equals the component's color. Clear the source components to the top background. Copy each component's exact mask at the same columns into its matching band, recolor it to that band's side-marker color, and shift it vertically so its lowest cell lands on the band's bottom row. Keep bands, dividers, and unmatched cells unchanged. This rule was validated on one demonstration and a correct test submission; verify the structural match before applying it, rather than treating it as a universal ARC rule.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global","evidence":"One demonstration pair and one correct test submission"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T04:08:25.500Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T04:08:25.500Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545240/attempts/f30eea74d21e478487daf9feda584c5e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260928044902807","summary":"Updated the global band-transfer memory with the validated component-wise projection rule.","rationale":"The task’s two demonstrations and correct solution show that each 4-connected component must be handled separately, including differently colored components within one visual motif; the earlier whole-template recoloring approach was incorrect.","expectedOutcome":"On matching puzzles, transfer only components whose colors match a band’s interior fill, preserving their masks and column positions, bottom-aligning them in the band, and recoloring them to its side-marker color; validate against the demonstrated component-wise pattern.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_components_into_matching_bands","title":"Transfer components into matching horizontal bands (general, v2)","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform-background upper workspace containing colored shapes and lower horizontal bands with distinct interior-fill and side-marker colors, process each upper-workspace foreground 4-connected component independently—even when different colors together form one visual motif. Match a component to a lower band when the component's color equals that band's interior-fill color. Project only that component's mask into the matching band at the same columns, bottom-align its bounding box within the band's height, and recolor its cells to the band's side-marker/border color. Leave all other cells in the component's bounding box as the band's fill color, preserve the band and unrelated cells, and clear the original foreground components from the upper workspace. Different colors within one motif can therefore populate different bands. Do not treat a whole multicolor template as one object or recolor its full bounding box. This rule was validated by two demonstrations and a correct solution for task-7c173794; an initial whole-template recoloring guess was incorrect.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Refines the existing band-transfer rule with the component-wise behavior directly validated by demonstrations and the solved task.","before":{"id":"arc_components_into_matching_bands","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: Transfer components into matching horizontal bands","content":"For ARC grids matching the demonstrated pattern—a uniform-background top workspace containing small colored components and lower horizontal bands with distinct fill colors and side-marker colors—match each top 4-connected component to the band whose fill color equals the component's color. Clear the source components to the top background. Copy each component's exact mask at the same columns into its matching band, recolor it to that band's side-marker color, and shift it vertically so its lowest cell lands on the band's bottom row. Keep bands, dividers, and unmatched cells unchanged. This rule was validated on one demonstration and a correct test submission; verify the structural match before applying it, rather than treating it as a universal ARC rule.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global","evidence":"One demonstration pair and one correct test submission"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T04:08:25.500Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T04:08:25.500Z","version":1},"after":{"id":"arc_components_into_matching_bands","kind":"memory","title":"Transfer components into matching horizontal bands (general, v2)","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform-background upper workspace containing colored shapes and lower horizontal bands with distinct interior-fill and side-marker colors, process each upper-workspace foreground 4-connected component independently—even when different colors together form one visual motif. Match a component to a lower band when the component's color equals that band's interior-fill color. Project only that component's mask into the matching band at the same columns, bottom-align its bounding box within the band's height, and recolor its cells to the band's side-marker/border color. Leave all other cells in the component's bounding box as the band's fill color, preserve the band and unrelated cells, and clear the original foreground components from the upper workspace. Different colors within one motif can therefore populate different bands. Do not treat a whole multicolor template as one object or recolor its full bounding box. This rule was validated by two demonstrations and a correct solution for task-7c173794; an initial whole-template recoloring guess was incorrect.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T04:08:25.500Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T04:49:08.434Z","version":2},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545240/attempts/f30eea74d21e478487daf9feda584c5e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260928051705058","summary":"Added a focused global ARC memory for marker-guided coloring of barrier-separated regions using 8-neighbor distance layers.","rationale":"The submitted 4-neighbor layer fill was incorrect, while the revised output using king-move distance layers solved the same instance; the demos support applying this rule only when that structure is confirmed.","expectedOutcome":"For matching ARC puzzles, identify free regions and marker colors by layer, use 8-neighbor distance layers, preserve barriers, and validate the rule against examples before applying it.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_marker_guided_chebyshev_region_layers","title":"ARC: Marker-guided coloring of barrier-separated regions with 8-neighbor layers","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background partitioned by line barriers and marker colors placed at successive inner boundary layers, first confirm this structure from the examples. Preserve barrier cells. Treat each 4-connected free region independently, find its boundary cells (adjacent by king move to a barrier or the grid exterior), and compute inward distance layers using 8-neighbor/king-move propagation within that region. Use the marker colors at successive depths as a cyclic color sequence for the layers, preserving any marker cells if the examples require it. A 4-neighbor BFS layer fill failed on the irregular region in task-9a643f12; the 8-neighbor layer version solved it. Apply only when examples confirm this rule, not as a general rule for arbitrary regions.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Records a validated, reusable ARC transformation and its important 4-neighbor versus 8-neighbor distinction.","after":{"id":"arc_marker_guided_chebyshev_region_layers","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: Marker-guided coloring of barrier-separated regions with 8-neighbor layers","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background partitioned by line barriers and marker colors placed at successive inner boundary layers, first confirm this structure from the examples. Preserve barrier cells. Treat each 4-connected free region independently, find its boundary cells (adjacent by king move to a barrier or the grid exterior), and compute inward distance layers using 8-neighbor/king-move propagation within that region. Use the marker colors at successive depths as a cyclic color sequence for the layers, preserving any marker cells if the examples require it. A 4-neighbor BFS layer fill failed on the irregular region in task-9a643f12; the 8-neighbor layer version solved it. Apply only when examples confirm this rule, not as a general rule for arbitrary regions.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T05:17:09.991Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T05:17:09.991Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545240/attempts/f30eea74d21e478487daf9feda584c5e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260928054835994","summary":"Created a global memory for repacking same-sized solid and hollow square tiles into two sorted columns.","rationale":"The demo establishes the arrangement and ordering rule, and the rule correctly predicted the solved test output.","expectedOutcome":"On matching ARC tasks, recognize repeated solid and hollow tiles, preserve their patterns, sort each group by original top-to-bottom position, and assemble corresponding tiles into rows with background padding where needed.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_repack_solid_and_hollow_square_tiles","title":"ARC: Repack solid and hollow square tiles into sorted columns","content":"For ARC grids containing repeated same-sized solid and hollow square tiles on a background, first verify the tile dimensions and that the objects separate into solid and hollow categories. Preserve each tile's cell pattern and color. Sort each category independently by its original top-to-bottom position. Build the output with hollow tiles in the left column and solid tiles in the right column, one tile per tile-height band; align category items by their sorted index and pad any missing tile with background. Infer output dimensions from the number of bands needed by the longer category. Apply only when the demonstrated structure fits.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"This rule was demonstrated on a seven-tile example and correctly predicted the solved test arrangement.","after":{"id":"arc_repack_solid_and_hollow_square_tiles","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: Repack solid and hollow square tiles into sorted columns","content":"For ARC grids containing repeated same-sized solid and hollow square tiles on a background, first verify the tile dimensions and that the objects separate into solid and hollow categories. Preserve each tile's cell pattern and color. Sort each category independently by its original top-to-bottom position. Build the output with hollow tiles in the left column and solid tiles in the right column, one tile per tile-height band; align category items by their sorted index and pad any missing tile with background. Infer output dimensions from the number of bands needed by the longer category. Apply only when the demonstrated structure fits.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T05:48:41.995Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T05:48:41.995Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545240/attempts/f30eea74d21e478487daf9feda584c5e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260928061721321","summary":"Created a focused global memory for the demonstrated ARC quadrant-stencil transformation.","rationale":"The demos and solved test support a reusable conditional rule: interpret the bottom-right quadrant as a stencil and use the bottom-left quadrant to fill its zero cells, with output dimensions or expansion depending on the demonstrated structure. The memory emphasizes validating the pattern before applying it.","expectedOutcome":"Future ARC solving should recognize matching quadrant-stencil examples and construct the output dimensions and fill values from the appropriate quadrants; validate against the examples before relying on the rule.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_quadrant_stencil_fill_and_expand","title":"ARC: Fill and expand a bottom-right quadrant stencil from bottom-left data","content":"For ARC inputs visibly divided into equal quadrants, consider whether the bottom-right quadrant is a repeating stencil whose zero cells are filled using the bottom-left quadrant. Confirm this structure against demonstrations before applying. In the demonstrated solid-data case, the bottom-left quadrant is a single solid color: tile the bottom-right pattern with its zero cells filled by that color, then crop to height equal to the count of nonzero cells in the top-left quadrant and width equal to the count of nonzero cells in the top-right quadrant. In the demonstrated varying-data case, bottom-left values supply fill colors for repeated bottom-right stencil tiles, producing an expanded output (the 8x8-to-16x16 example). A solved test had 16 ones in the top-left, 4 fives in the top-right, solid 9 in the bottom-left, and a 3/0 checkerboard stencil; its output was a 16x4 checkerboard of 3 and 9. One demo with an all-zero bottom-right produced the bottom-left pattern as output, but treat that only as an observed fallback, not a general rule.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The demonstrations and solved test establish a conditional, reusable quadrant-stencil pattern, while also showing that structural validation is necessary.","after":{"id":"arc_quadrant_stencil_fill_and_expand","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: Fill and expand a bottom-right quadrant stencil from bottom-left data","content":"For ARC inputs visibly divided into equal quadrants, consider whether the bottom-right quadrant is a repeating stencil whose zero cells are filled using the bottom-left quadrant. Confirm this structure against demonstrations before applying. In the demonstrated solid-data case, the bottom-left quadrant is a single solid color: tile the bottom-right pattern with its zero cells filled by that color, then crop to height equal to the count of nonzero cells in the top-left quadrant and width equal to the count of nonzero cells in the top-right quadrant. In the demonstrated varying-data case, bottom-left values supply fill colors for repeated bottom-right stencil tiles, producing an expanded output (the 8x8-to-16x16 example). A solved test had 16 ones in the top-left, 4 fives in the top-right, solid 9 in the bottom-left, and a 3/0 checkerboard stencil; its output was a 16x4 checkerboard of 3 and 9. One demo with an all-zero bottom-right produced the bottom-left pattern as output, but treat that only as an observed fallback, not a general rule.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T06:17:30.268Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T06:17:30.269Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545240/attempts/f30eea74d21e478487daf9feda584c5e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260928062006944","summary":"Create a focused global memory for the demonstrated ARC quadrant-mask expansion rule.","rationale":"The task was solved correctly, and the requested rule captures a reusable transformation supported by the demonstrated quadrant structure.","expectedOutcome":"On future grids matching this structure, infer the output dimensions and expand the bottom-left source through the bottom-right mask; validate the pattern against available examples before applying it.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_quadrant_mask_expansion","title":"ARC: Expand bottom-left source through bottom-right mask using top-quadrant counts","content":"For even-sized ARC grids that visibly divide into four equal quadrants, consider this rule only when the upper quadrants encode output dimensions and the lower quadrants form a source-and-mask pair. Count nonzero cells in the top-left quadrant for output height and in the top-right quadrant for output width. Use the bottom-left as the source and the bottom-right as a block mask: expand each source cell into a bottom-right-sized block, preserving nonzero mask colors and replacing mask zeros with that source cell's color, then crop to the counted output dimensions. If the bottom-right is entirely zero, the demonstrated variant is a direct crop of the bottom-left. Confirm the quadrant roles and output dimensions against examples before applying.","metadata":{"scope":"global","evidence":"Validated by a correct submission on task-b4e6b93d"},"reason":"Preserves the requested, task-validated transformation as a narrowly scoped reusable ARC pattern.","after":{"id":"arc_quadrant_mask_expansion","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: Expand bottom-left source through bottom-right mask using top-quadrant counts","content":"For even-sized ARC grids that visibly divide into four equal quadrants, consider this rule only when the upper quadrants encode output dimensions and the lower quadrants form a source-and-mask pair. Count nonzero cells in the top-left quadrant for output height and in the top-right quadrant for output width. Use the bottom-left as the source and the bottom-right as a block mask: expand each source cell into a bottom-right-sized block, preserving nonzero mask colors and replacing mask zeros with that source cell's color, then crop to the counted output dimensions. If the bottom-right is entirely zero, the demonstrated variant is a direct crop of the bottom-left. Confirm the quadrant roles and output dimensions against examples before applying.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global","evidence":"Validated by a correct submission on task-b4e6b93d"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T06:20:16.770Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T06:20:16.770Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545240/attempts/f30eea74d21e478487daf9feda584c5e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260928075311696","summary":"Added a global ARC memory for the demonstrated four-quadrant tile-expansion rule.","rationale":"Three demonstrations support the rule: output dimensions equal the nonzero-cell counts in the upper-left and upper-right quadrants; the lower-right quadrant repeats to fill that output, preserving its nonzero cells and replacing its zeros with values selected from the lower-left quadrant by tile coordinates. The rule also produced the correct 3×4 solution for task-b4e6b93d.","expectedOutcome":"For future matching ARC grids, recognize and apply the quadrant-count and tile-fill transformation; validate the inferred dimensions and cell mapping against examples before using it.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_four_quadrant_count_guided_tile_expansion","title":"ARC: Four-quadrant count-guided tile expansion","content":"For even-sized ARC grids that visibly split into four equal quadrants, consider this rule only when the examples support it: the output height is the number of nonzero cells in the upper-left quadrant, and the output width is the number of nonzero cells in the upper-right quadrant. Tile the lower-right quadrant periodically to those dimensions. Preserve its nonzero cells; for each zero at output coordinate (r,c), fill it with the lower-left quadrant cell at (r // q, c // q), where q is the quadrant side length. Validate the quadrant structure, dimensions, and cell mapping against available examples before applying.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"This reusable transformation is supported by three demonstrations and solved the current test instance.","after":{"id":"arc_four_quadrant_count_guided_tile_expansion","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: Four-quadrant count-guided tile expansion","content":"For even-sized ARC grids that visibly split into four equal quadrants, consider this rule only when the examples support it: the output height is the number of nonzero cells in the upper-left quadrant, and the output width is the number of nonzero cells in the upper-right quadrant. Tile the lower-right quadrant periodically to those dimensions. Preserve its nonzero cells; for each zero at output coordinate (r,c), fill it with the lower-left quadrant cell at (r // q, c // q), where q is the quadrant side length. Validate the quadrant structure, dimensions, and cell mapping against available examples before applying.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T07:53:17.969Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T07:53:17.969Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545240/attempts/f30eea74d21e478487daf9feda584c5e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260928082916316","summary":"Create a focused global memory for the demonstrated ARC bar-and-snake pattern, with cautious routing guidance.","rationale":"The demos support changing color-1 cells in the bars to color 2 and drawing an orthogonal color-3 route around the bars. They suggest route offsets are related to the number of color-1 markers per bar, but do not justify treating the proposed endpoint-clearance rule as universal; the memory should require validating the exact orientation and offsets against the input.","expectedOutcome":"Future solving can recognize this pattern while avoiding unsupported assumptions about route geometry; validate by checking the inferred path against the bar orientations, marker counts, seed, and available examples.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_marker_count_guided_bar_snake_route","title":"ARC: Complete marked bars and route a color-3 snake (general, v1)","content":"For ARC grids matching the demonstrated setup—background 8, straight bar segments made of colors 1 and 2, and a single color-3 seed—consider completing the bars by changing their color-1 cells to color 2, then drawing an orthogonal color-3 snake around the bars toward the opposite edge. In the examples, route offsets are related to the number of color-1 markers on each bar (observed distances correspond to marker count plus one), and the route alternates direction around successive bars. Treat that offset as a hypothesis: first determine the bar orientation and order, then verify the specific bends and distances against all available examples. Do not assume a fixed route or apply this rule to unrelated grids.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Persist the requested reusable pattern while qualifying the less-certain clearance rule to match the demonstration evidence.","after":{"id":"arc_marker_count_guided_bar_snake_route","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: Complete marked bars and route a color-3 snake (general, v1)","content":"For ARC grids matching the demonstrated setup—background 8, straight bar segments made of colors 1 and 2, and a single color-3 seed—consider completing the bars by changing their color-1 cells to color 2, then drawing an orthogonal color-3 snake around the bars toward the opposite edge. In the examples, route offsets are related to the number of color-1 markers on each bar (observed distances correspond to marker count plus one), and the route alternates direction around successive bars. Treat that offset as a hypothesis: first determine the bar orientation and order, then verify the specific bends and distances against all available examples. Do not assume a fixed route or apply this rule to unrelated grids.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T08:29:26.078Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T08:29:26.079Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545240/attempts/f30eea74d21e478487daf9feda584c5e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260928083127541","summary":"Added a focused global memory for the verified framed-object stacking transformation.","rationale":"The rule was supported by a demonstration and correctly solved the test: with an 8 background and color-1 top/color-2 left frame, separated 2-row-high payload objects were read row-major, preserved, and stacked in a canvas sized to the widest object.","expectedOutcome":"For future matching ARC grids, recognize the frame and payload layout, preserve object patterns and order, and center narrower objects with 8-padding; validate that the structural conditions hold before applying the rule.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_framed_row_major_object_stacking","title":"ARC: Stack row-major objects from a framed workspace","content":"For ARC grids matching the demonstrated structure, with background 8, a color-1 top frame, a color-2 left frame, and payload made of separated 2-row-high colored objects, read objects row-major (top to bottom, left to right), preserve each object's pattern, and stack them vertically. Use a canvas as wide as the widest object, centering narrower objects with 8-padding. Confirm the frame and payload layout before applying; do not generalize to other frames or object shapes without evidence.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"This focused rule matched a demonstration and correctly solved the test instance.","after":{"id":"arc_framed_row_major_object_stacking","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: Stack row-major objects from a framed workspace","content":"For ARC grids matching the demonstrated structure, with background 8, a color-1 top frame, a color-2 left frame, and payload made of separated 2-row-high colored objects, read objects row-major (top to bottom, left to right), preserve each object's pattern, and stack them vertically. Use a canvas as wide as the widest object, centering narrower objects with 8-padding. Confirm the frame and payload layout before applying; do not 