{"id":"refine_20260928092612116","summary":"Added a focused global memory for the demonstrated color-2 connector assembly pattern.","rationale":"Two demonstrations and a correct test submission support a reusable rule: keep component orientations fixed and assemble pieces by translating them until their color-2 connector markers pair, with the solid color-4 component serving as the anchor in these examples.","expectedOutcome":"For future puzzles with the same structure, identify components and adjacent color-2 markers, infer compatible translations, and validate the assembled output for paired markers and non-overlapping pieces without applying the rule to unrelated layouts.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_color2_connector_component_assembly","title":"ARC: Assemble components by matching color-2 connectors","content":"For ARC grids matching the demonstrated connector-assembly pattern, first verify that colored pieces are rigid components accompanied by color-2 connector cells. Identify each component and record the coordinates of its adjacent color-2 markers; preserve each piece's orientation. In the demonstrated examples, keep the solid color-4 component as the anchor. Find translations that make connector-marker sets from different pieces overlap, using alignments of at least two markers as strong candidates. Assemble a connected chain while favoring complete one-to-one pairing of all connector markers and avoiding overlaps between colored piece cells. Preserve the piece colors and marker cells in the output, and validate the resulting placements and grid dimensions. Treat the anchor, chain structure, and matching rule as specific to grids that visibly support this pattern; do not generalize to unrelated marker layouts.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The connector assembly rule was supported by two demonstration pairs and a correct test submission.","after":{"id":"arc_color2_connector_component_assembly","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: Assemble components by matching color-2 connectors","content":"For ARC grids matching the demonstrated connector-assembly pattern, first verify that colored pieces are rigid components accompanied by color-2 connector cells. Identify each component and record the coordinates of its adjacent color-2 markers; preserve each piece's orientation. In the demonstrated examples, keep the solid color-4 component as the anchor. Find translations that make connector-marker sets from different pieces overlap, using alignments of at least two markers as strong candidates. Assemble a connected chain while favoring complete one-to-one pairing of all connector markers and avoiding overlaps between colored piece cells. Preserve the piece colors and marker cells in the output, and validate the resulting placements and grid dimensions. Treat the anchor, chain structure, and matching rule as specific to grids that visibly support this pattern; do not generalize to unrelated marker layouts.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T09:26:17.257Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T09:26:17.257Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545240/attempts/f30eea74d21e478487daf9feda584c5e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260928102007801","summary":"Created a global memory for the verified ARC masked-canvas fill transformation.","rationale":"Three demonstrations and a correct submission support this reusable rule: copy palette-object patterns into a matching-background mask while preserving the target canvas’s base cells.","expectedOutcome":"Future solving can recognize similar canvas-and-palette layouts, infer piece placement from mask geometry, and validate coverage using object shapes and area conservation.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_masked_canvas_fill_from_object_palette","title":"ARC: Fill a masked canvas from palette objects","content":"For ARC grids where one panel is a canvas containing a base color and mask cells, and a separate palette panel has that mask color as its background, consider replacing the canvas mask cells with foreground patterns copied from palette objects. Confirm the panel roles and color relationship from the input and demonstrations; do not assume a particular color or panel orientation. Treat each palette object as its connected non-background pattern, preserving all internal marker colors. Partition or tile these patterns to cover the mask exactly, using object dimensions, geometry, and area conservation to infer placement. Preserve all base-color canvas cells. Validate that every mask cell is covered and that copied patterns retain their shapes and colors. Verified example: a 15×7 canvas with base color 4 and 41 mask cells of color 7 was filled using palette objects 1 (2×5), 6 (6×2), 2 (2×2), two 8 (1×4), 3 (3×2), and 0 (singleton).","metadata":{"scope":"global","evidence":"Three demonstrations and one correct submission"},"reason":"This is an explicitly requested, evidence-backed reusable ARC transformation.","after":{"id":"arc_masked_canvas_fill_from_object_palette","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: Fill a masked canvas from palette objects","content":"For ARC grids where one panel is a canvas containing a base color and mask cells, and a separate palette panel has that mask color as its background, consider replacing the canvas mask cells with foreground patterns copied from palette objects. Confirm the panel roles and color relationship from the input and demonstrations; do not assume a particular color or panel orientation. Treat each palette object as its connected non-background pattern, preserving all internal marker colors. Partition or tile these patterns to cover the mask exactly, using object dimensions, geometry, and area conservation to infer placement. Preserve all base-color canvas cells. Validate that every mask cell is covered and that copied patterns retain their shapes and colors. Verified example: a 15×7 canvas with base color 4 and 41 mask cells of color 7 was filled using palette objects 1 (2×5), 6 (6×2), 2 (2×2), two 8 (1×4), 3 (3×2), and 0 (singleton).","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global","evidence":"Three demonstrations and one correct submission"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T10:20:12.649Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T10:20:12.649Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545240/attempts/f30eea74d21e478487daf9feda584c5e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
