the structure. Preserve color-7 cells on the marker column. Extend color 2 from the edge marker along that column through blanks until the first color-7 cell is encountered; preserve any color-7 cells on the axis. Split the off-axis color-7 cells into left and right patterns and translate each pattern rigidly to its corresponding corner on the marker's edge (top-left and top-right for a top marker; bottom-left and bottom-right for a bottom marker), retaining each pattern's cell arrangement. This rule is supported by a bottom-marker demonstration and a correctly submitted top-marker test. Do not generalize to cases with multiple markers based on this evidence.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T10:50:46.712Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T10:50:46.712Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545240/attempts/f30eea74d21e478487daf9feda584c5e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260928130623105","summary":"Added a global ARC memory for the demonstrated framed-piece extraction and stacking rule.","rationale":"Two demonstrations and a correct test submission support ordering pieces according to the frame colors, rotating tall pieces to horizontal, and stacking them in a padded canvas; structural safeguards limit application to matching layouts.","expectedOutcome":"Future solving can recognize matching framed-piece layouts, infer piece order and orientation, and validate the stacked output dimensions and padding against the input.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_framed_piece_order_rotate_and_stack","title":"ARC: Order, rotate, and stack pieces from a color-1/color-2 frame","content":"For ARC grids matching the demonstrated layout, identify the color-8 background, color-1/color-2 top-and-left frame, and the separated foreground pieces inside the frame. Treat each piece as a connected non-background object, excluding frame cells. Order pieces row-major when the top frame is color 1 and the left frame is color 2; order column-major when the top is color 2 and the left is color 1. Rotate any tall piece 90 degrees counterclockwise so its long side is horizontal. Stack pieces vertically in the inferred order, centering each in a canvas whose width is the largest resulting piece width and padding with background 8. Before applying, verify the frame orientation, object separation/connectivity, background, and ordering against the examples; preserve each piece's cell pattern and colors.","metadata":{"scope":"global","evidence":"Two demonstrations and a correct test submission"},"reason":"Persist the verified reusable transformation with explicit applicability checks.","after":{"id":"arc_framed_piece_order_rotate_and_stack","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: Order, rotate, and stack pieces from a color-1/color-2 frame","content":"For ARC grids matching the demonstrated layout, identify the color-8 background, color-1/color-2 top-and-left frame, and the separated foreground pieces inside the frame. Treat each piece as a connected non-background object, excluding frame cells. Order pieces row-major when the top frame is color 1 and the left frame is color 2; order column-major when the top is color 2 and the left is color 1. Rotate any tall piece 90 degrees counterclockwise so its long side is horizontal. Stack pieces vertically in the inferred order, centering each in a canvas whose width is the largest resulting piece width and padding with background 8. Before applying, verify the frame orientation, object separation/connectivity, background, and ordering against the examples; preserve each piece's cell pattern and colors.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global","evidence":"Two demonstrations and a correct test submission"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T13:06:30.067Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T13:06:30.067Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545240/attempts/f30eea74d21e478487daf9feda584c5e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260928152605888","summary":"Added a global memory for the verified ARC transformation that sorts hollow 4×4 frames and solid 4×4 blocks into side-by-side columns.","rationale":"The rule was supported by a demonstration and a correctly solved test: each class is ordered top-to-bottom, frames occupy the left column, solids the right, and the shorter column is zero-padded.","expectedOutcome":"For future matching grids, recognize and apply this repacking rule while verifying the tile sizes, object classes, and within-class vertical order before transforming.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_sort_and_repack_hollow_frames_and_solid_blocks","title":"ARC: Sort and repack hollow frames and solid blocks","content":"For ARC grids containing scattered 4×4 hollow square frames and solid square blocks, first verify that these are the two object classes and that their cell patterns and dimensions match. Extract the objects, sort each class independently by top-to-bottom position in the input, and arrange one 4×4 tile per row in a two-column output: hollow frames on the left and solid blocks on the right. Preserve each object's color and pattern, including zero interiors in frames. If the columns contain different numbers of objects, pad the shorter column with 4×4 zeros. Evidence: a demonstration with frames 3,9 and solid blocks 1,8,5 produced a 12×8 output; a test with frames 8,2,9 and solid blocks 1,3,4,7 produced a 16×8 output and was confirmed correct. Caution: verify both object classes and their top-to-bottom orders before applying; do not generalize to different tile sizes or layouts without evidence.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"A demonstrated rule also correctly solved the test, making this a reusable, evidence-backed ARC transformation.","after":{"id":"arc_sort_and_repack_hollow_frames_and_solid_blocks","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: Sort and repack hollow frames and solid blocks","content":"For ARC grids containing scattered 4×4 hollow square frames and solid square blocks, first verify that these are the two object classes and that their cell patterns and dimensions match. Extract the objects, sort each class independently by top-to-bottom position in the input, and arrange one 4×4 tile per row in a two-column output: hollow frames on the left and solid blocks on the right. Preserve each object's color and pattern, including zero interiors in frames. If the columns contain different numbers of objects, pad the shorter column with 4×4 zeros. Evidence: a demonstration with frames 3,9 and solid blocks 1,8,5 produced a 12×8 output; a test with frames 8,2,9 and solid blocks 1,3,4,7 produced a 16×8 output and was confirmed correct. Caution: verify both object classes and their top-to-bottom orders before applying; do not generalize to different tile sizes or layouts without evidence.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T15:26:11.090Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T15:26:11.090Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545240/attempts/f30eea74d21e478487daf9feda584c5e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260928153212068","summary":"Added a global ARC memory for the verified framed two-lane strip-compaction transformation.","rationale":"The demo and correctly solved transposed test support this reusable rule, including canonicalizing swapped guide orientation, processing chunks in lane order, and compacting their rotated forms.","expectedOutcome":"For future grids matching this framed strip structure, identify and compact the chunks in the demonstrated order while preserving their colors; validate the frame, lane widths, and chunk boundaries before applying the rule.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_two_lane_strip_compaction","title":"ARC: Compact framed two-lane strips by rotating and stacking chunks","content":"For ARC grids with a color-8 background, color-1/color-2 guide borders, and separated vertical strips two cells wide, first verify the guide and strip structure. If the guide orientation is swapped, transpose the grid to canonicalize it to color 2 on top and color 1 on the left. Remove the guide border and identify the vertical chunks in the two strips. Process chunks top-to-bottom in the left strip, then top-to-bottom in the right strip. Rotate each chunk 90 degrees counterclockwise and vertically concatenate the results without gaps. Center each rotated chunk horizontally in a color-8 output whose width is the maximum chunk height. Preserve every cell color, including marker cells. Evidence: one demo pair and a correctly solved transposed test instance; confirm the structure before applying.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Records a demonstrated reusable ARC transformation that solved a test instance after handling transposed guide orientation.","after":{"id":"arc_two_lane_strip_compaction","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: Compact framed two-lane strips by rotating and stacking chunks","content":"For ARC grids with a color-8 background, color-1/color-2 guide borders, and separated vertical strips two cells wide, first verify the guide and strip structure. If the guide orientation is swapped, transpose the grid to canonicalize it to color 2 on top and color 1 on the left. Remove the guide border and identify the vertical chunks in the two strips. Process chunks top-to-bottom in the left strip, then top-to-bottom in the right strip. Rotate each chunk 90 degrees counterclockwise and vertically concatenate the results without gaps. Center each rotated chunk horizontally in a color-8 output whose width is the maximum chunk height. Preserve every cell color, including marker cells. Evidence: one demo pair and a correctly solved transposed test instance; confirm the structure before applying.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T15:32:18.414Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T15:32:18.414Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545240/attempts/f30eea74d21e478487daf9feda584c5e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260928155550359","summary":"Added a global memory for the demonstrated matching-color tray transformation.","rationale":"The demo supports a reusable rule: route each upper-region 4-connected component to the tray whose interior matches its original color, bottom-align it while preserving its shape and columns, and recolor it to the tray’s side-rail color. The rule correctly predicted the test output.","expectedOutcome":"Future solving can recognize matching tray layouts, transform only when the structure fits, and verify component placement, alignment, and recoloring against the grid.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_components_into_matching_trays","title":"ARC: Transfer components into matching-color trays","content":"For ARC grids with an upper workspace containing colored components and lower horizontal trays with uniform interior-fill colors and distinct side-rail colors, first verify the layout and component/tray color matches. Route each upper-workspace foreground 4-connected component independently to the tray whose interior fill matches the component’s original color. Preserve the component’s shape and columns, bottom-align it within the tray, and recolor its cells to that tray’s side-rail color. Components of different colors within a motif route separately. Clear the source components to the upper workspace background; preserve tray cells not occupied by transferred components and all unrelated grid regions. Validate tray capacity, component placement, and recoloring before applying.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The matching-color tray rule was demonstrated and correctly predicted the test output, supporting a focused reusable ARC memory.","after":{"id":"arc_components_into_matching_trays","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: Transfer components into matching-color trays","content":"For ARC grids with an upper workspace containing colored components and lower horizontal trays with uniform interior-fill colors and distinct side-rail colors, first verify the layout and component/tray color matches. Route each upper-workspace foreground 4-connected component independently to the tray whose interior fill matches the component’s original color. Preserve the component’s shape and columns, bottom-align it within the tray, and recolor its cells to that tray’s side-rail color. Components of different colors within a motif route separately. Clear the source components to the upper workspace background; preserve tray cells not occupied by transferred components and all unrelated grid regions. Validate tray capacity, component placement, and recoloring before applying.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T15:55:55.052Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T15:55:55.052Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545240/attempts/f30eea74d21e478487daf9feda584c5e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260928163321729","summary":"Added a focused global ARC memory for converting a touching chain of colored components into a one-column color sequence.","rationale":"The demo and correctly solved test support the same transformation: list the chain’s component colors from the upper-left endpoint and repeat each color according to that component’s cell count.","expectedOutcome":"For future matching grids, recognize the chain structure, preserve its demonstrated order, and verify the output height equals the total number of foreground cells before submitting.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_touching_color_chain_to_column","title":"ARC: Flatten a touching chain of colored components into a column","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background and a visibly single chain of differently colored monochrome 4-connected components, transform the chain into a one-column output. Read components in chain order starting from the upper-left endpoint, and repeat each component’s color once per cell in that component. The demonstrated example had counts 7×3, 5×5, 6×5, 8×4, 9×5; the solved test had 8×2, 9×3, 5×6, 1×6, 8×3, 9×5. Apply only when the single touching-chain structure and endpoint/order are supported; count each component’s cells and verify the output height equals the total foreground-cell count.","metadata":{"scope":"global","task_id":"task-b8158d8c"},"reason":"A demo validated the chain-to-column rule, and the test was solved correctly with the same component-order and cell-count procedure.","after":{"id":"arc_touching_color_chain_to_column","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: Flatten a touching chain of colored components into a column","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background and a visibly single chain of differently colored monochrome 4-connected components, transform the chain into a one-column output. Read components in chain order starting from the upper-left endpoint, and repeat each component’s color once per cell in that component. The demonstrated example had counts 7×3, 5×5, 6×5, 8×4, 9×5; the solved test had 8×2, 9×3, 5×6, 1×6, 8×3, 9×5. Apply only when the single touching-chain structure and endpoint/order are supported; count each component’s cells and verify the output height equals the total foreground-cell count.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global","task_id":"task-b8158d8c"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T16:33:25.982Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T16:33:25.982Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545240/attempts/f30eea74d21e478487daf9feda584c5e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260928163441906","summary":"Added a focused global ARC memory for resizing parallel straight strokes to a color-8 reference length.","rationale":"The demo and solved test support using the color-8 stroke as a length reference and anchoring matching parallel strokes at their lower-row endpoint; the rule should be applied only when the stroke orientation and structure match.","expectedOutcome":"On structurally matching ARC tasks, identify the reference length and orientation, resize eligible parallel strokes while preserving their lower-row endpoints, and verify the result against the demonstrated pattern.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_parallel_stroke_resize_to_color8_reference","title":"ARC: Resize parallel strokes to a color-8 reference length","content":"For ARC grids matching the demonstrated pattern, use a straight color-8 stroke as the reference length and resize other straight strokes parallel to it to that length, preserving each resized stroke's endpoint with the greatest row coordinate. Confirm that the strokes share the same orientation, identify the full reference stroke and its length, and verify which colored strokes are eligible before transforming. The demonstrations include diagonal strokes and vertical strokes; do not infer that unrelated or nonparallel objects should be changed.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The demo and correctly solved test provide evidence for a reusable, orientation-aware stroke-length rule with lower-row endpoint anchoring.","after":{"id":"arc_parallel_stroke_resize_to_color8_reference","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: Resize parallel strokes to a color-8 reference length","content":"For ARC grids matching the demonstrated pattern, use a straight color-8 stroke as the reference length and resize other straight strokes parallel to it to that length, preserving each resized stroke's endpoint with the greatest row coordinate. Confirm that the strokes share the same orientation, identify the full reference stroke and its length, and verify which colored strokes are eligible before transforming. The demonstrations include diagonal strokes and vertical strokes; do not infer that unrelated or nonparallel objects should be changed.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T16:34:51.544Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T16:34:51.544Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545240/attempts/f30eea74d21e478487daf9feda584c5e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260928175816713","summary":"Created a focused global memory for reordering color-6-separated ARC panels into a chain by matching edge occupancy masks.","rationale":"The demos and solved test support a reusable, pattern-specific rule: connect panels by matching foreground/background occupancy along adjoining edges, independent of foreground colors, while preserving each panel’s contents and orientation.","expectedOutcome":"For structurally matching grids, the agent can infer the panel chain from edge profiles, preserve separators and panel data, and validate the proposed order and blank outer-facing end edges against the input.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_reorder_color6_panels_by_edge_occupancy","title":"ARC: Reorder color-6-separated panels by matching edge occupancy","content":"For ARC grids with background 7 partitioned by color-6 lines into equal square panels, consider whether the panels must be reordered into a single chain. Apply this rule only when the panel structure and demonstrations support it. For each panel, record binary foreground/background occupancy profiles along all four edges; match adjoining panels by these profiles, regardless of foreground color. Find an ordering that gives blank outer-facing start/end edges when supported by the examples. Preserve every panel's cells, colors, and orientation, and retain color-6 separators between panels. The demonstrated cases included horizontal, vertical, and 2×2 panel layouts; the solved test chained bottom panel → middle panel → top panel. Verify extracted panel boundaries, edge profiles, chain order, output orientation, and separator placement before submitting; do not infer this rule from color-6 lines alone.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The solved instance and four demonstrations provide evidence for a reusable but narrowly scoped panel-reordering rule.","after":{"id":"arc_reorder_color6_panels_by_edge_occupancy","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: Reorder color-6-separated panels by matching edge occupancy","content":"For ARC grids with background 7 partitioned by color-6 lines into equal square panels, consider whether the panels must be reordered into a single chain. Apply this rule only when the panel structure and demonstrations support it. For each panel, record binary foreground/background occupancy profiles along all four edges; match adjoining panels by these profiles, regardless of foreground color. Find an ordering that gives blank outer-facing start/end edges when supported by the examples. Preserve every panel's cells, colors, and orientation, and retain color-6 separators between panels. The demonstrated cases included horizontal, vertical, and 2×2 panel layouts; the solved test chained bottom panel → middle panel → top panel. Verify extracted panel boundaries, edge profiles, chain order, output orientation, and separator placement before submitting; do not infer this rule from color-6 lines alone.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T17:58:22.196Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T17:58:22.196Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545240/attempts/f30eea74d21e478487daf9feda584c5e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260928180954381","summary":"Created a global ARC memory for transferring matching 3×3 motifs into the centers of solid 5×5 color panels.","rationale":"The demo and correctly solved test support a reusable rule: each panel is patterned by its matching-color motif, with motif-colored cells changed to the background color.","expectedOutcome":"For future structurally matching grids, identify the panel mosaic and matching motifs, apply each motif to the corresponding panel center, and verify panel arrangement, gutters, and border are preserved.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_matching_motif_to_solid_panel","title":"ARC: Pattern solid panels using matching-color motifs","content":"For ARC grids with solid 5×5 color panels separated by one-cell background gutters and an outer border, plus matching 3×3 motifs made of panel colors on the same background, use each color's motif to pattern its corresponding panel. Replace the center 3×3 cells of each panel with the background color wherever the matching motif contains that panel's color; leave the panel color in the other center cells. Preserve the panel arrangement, gutters, and border. First verify the panel and motif dimensions, background color, and color correspondence. Supported by a demo with panels of colors 2, 5, and 9 and a correctly solved test with a 2×2 arrangement of colors 9, 6, 3, and 4.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The transformation was validated by a demonstration and a correctly solved test instance, making it a focused reusable ARC pattern.","after":{"id":"arc_matching_motif_to_solid_panel","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: Pattern solid panels using matching-color motifs","content":"For ARC grids with solid 5×5 color panels separated by one-cell background gutters and an outer border, plus matching 3×3 motifs made of panel colors on the same background, use each color's motif to pattern its corresponding panel. Replace the center 3×3 cells of each panel with the background color wherever the matching motif contains that panel's color; leave the panel color in the other center cells. Preserve the panel arrangement, gutters, and border. First verify the panel and motif dimensions, background color, and color correspondence. Supported by a demo with panels of colors 2, 5, and 9 and a correctly solved test with a 2×2 arrangement of colors 9, 6, 3, and 4.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T18:09:59.660Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T18:09:59.660Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545240/attempts/f30eea74d21e478487daf9feda584c5e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260928181430931","summary":"Created a global ARC memory for resizing aligned parallel bars to their median length while preserving their shared endpoint.","rationale":"Two demonstrated orientations support the same rule: horizontal bars were right-aligned and resized to the median length, and vertical bars were bottom-aligned and resized to the median length; the vertical-bar test submission was correct.","expectedOutcome":"On future puzzles with matching structure, the agent can resize each bar to the median bar length while preserving orientation, location, color, and the shared endpoint, and verify those conditions against the input.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_parallel_bars_to_median_length_at_shared_endpoint","title":"ARC: Resize aligned parallel bars to median length at shared endpoint","content":"For ARC grids with several straight, parallel bars of differing lengths that share an aligned endpoint, first confirm this structure and that the demonstrations support median-length normalization. Find the median of the bar lengths and resize every bar to that length, preserving each bar's orientation, location/line, color, and shared endpoint. Thus right-aligned horizontal bars retain their right endpoint; bottom-aligned vertical bars retain their bottom endpoint. Apply only when the shared-endpoint pattern matches; do not infer this rule for unrelated strokes.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The same rule is evidenced by horizontal and vertical demonstrations, and the vertical case was validated by a correct test submission.","after":{"id":"arc_parallel_bars_to_median_length_at_shared_endpoint","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: Resize aligned parallel bars to median length at shared endpoint","content":"For ARC grids with several straight, parallel bars of differing lengths that share an aligned endpoint, first confirm this structure and that the demonstrations support median-length normalization. Find the median of the bar lengths and resize every bar to that length, preserving each bar's orientation, location/line, color, and shared endpoint. Thus right-aligned horizontal bars retain their right endpoint; bottom-aligned vertical bars retain their bottom endpoint. Apply only when the shared-endpoint pattern matches; do not infer this rule for unrelated strokes.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T18:14:37.143Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T18:14:37.143Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545240/attempts/f30eea74d21e478487daf9feda584c5e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260928185615439","summary":"Created a global ARC memory for stacking separated tiles according to the orientation of the top and left guide borders.","rationale":"Two demonstration pairs and a correctly solved test support the guide-directed ordering, tile orientation, centering, and background-padding rules; the memory is explicitly limited to matching layouts.","expectedOutcome":"For future matching tasks, infer tile order from the guide borders, orient and center tiles consistently, and validate the resulting stack against the input pieces.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_guide_directed_tile_stack","title":"ARC: Stack separated tiles in guide-directed order","content":"For ARC grids with background 8, homogeneous top and left guide borders colored 1 and 2, and separated rectangular tile pieces, apply this rule only when the layout matches the demonstrated examples. Extract the pieces and stack them vertically in guide-directed order: top border 1 with left border 2 means row-major order (top bands, left-to-right); top border 2 with left border 1 means column-major order (left-to-right lanes, top-to-bottom). Keep landscape tiles horizontal; rotate portrait tiles 90 degrees counterclockwise so their long side is horizontal; leave square tiles unchanged. Set output width to the widest oriented tile, center each tile with background-8 padding, and concatenate tiles from top to bottom. Verify that tile patterns/colors and total output height are preserved.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The rule is supported by two demonstrations and a correctly solved test instance, and is specific enough to help with recurring matching ARC tasks.","after":{"id":"arc_guide_directed_tile_stack","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: Stack separated tiles in guide-directed order","content":"For ARC grids with background 8, homogeneous top and left guide borders colored 1 and 2, and separated rectangular tile pieces, apply this rule only when the layout matches the demonstrated examples. Extract the pieces and stack them vertically in guide-directed order: top border 1 with left border 2 means row-major order (top bands, left-to-right); top border 2 with left border 1 means column-major order (left-to-right lanes, top-to-bottom). Keep landscape tiles horizontal; rotate portrait tiles 90 degrees counterclockwise so their long side is horizontal; leave square tiles unchanged. Set output width to the widest oriented tile, center each tile with background-8 padding, and concatenate tiles from top to bottom. Verify that tile patterns/colors and total output height are preserved.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T18:56:22.807Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T18:56:22.807Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545240/attempts/f30eea74d21e478487daf9feda584c5e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260928193837014","summary":"Added a focused global ARC memory for arranging separated panels into a strip by matching boundary occupancy profiles.","rationale":"The solved instance supports reordering unrotated 5×5 panels by matching non-background edge profiles, while preserving separator bars; the demos also show panels assembled into strips. This is a reusable tactic, provided it is applied only when the panel structure and a unique chain support it.","expectedOutcome":"On structurally matching panel puzzles, compare edge occupancy profiles to infer a supported strip order, preserve panel contents and separators, and validate the chain before submitting.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_assemble_panels_into_strip_by_edge_occupancy","title":"ARC: Assemble separated panels into a strip by matching edge occupancy","content":"For ARC grids visibly divided into equal square panels by full separator lines, consider assembling the panels into a one-dimensional strip by matching their edge occupancy profiles. For each panel, record the occupied/non-background pattern along each of its four boundary edges, ignoring foreground color. Reorder panels so adjacent facing profiles line up; when available, use blank unmatched profiles to identify the strip endpoints. Preserve each panel's cells and orientation, and retain separator lines between panels in the output. Apply only when the panel structure is clear and the edge evidence supports a unique or strongly supported chain; otherwise do not assume this transformation. Validate the resulting order and output dimensions against the examples.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"A matching edge-profile chain recovered the correct panel order in the solved instance after two unsupported orders failed, and demo pairs provide additional examples of panel strips.","after":{"id":"arc_assemble_panels_into_strip_by_edge_occupancy","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: Assemble separated panels into a strip by matching edge occupancy","content":"For ARC grids visibly divided into equal square panels by full separator lines, consider assembling the panels into a one-dimensional strip by matching their edge occupancy profiles. For each panel, record the occupied/non-background pattern along each of its four boundary edges, ignoring foreground color. Reorder panels so adjacent facing profiles line up; when available, use blank unmatched profiles to identify the strip endpoints. Preserve each panel's cells and orientation, and retain separator lines between panels in the output. Apply only when the panel structure is clear and the edge evidence supports a unique or strongly supported chain; otherwise do not assume this transformation. Validate the resulting order and output dimensions against the examples.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T19:38:45.323Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T19:38:45.323Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545240/attempts/f30eea74d21e478487daf9feda584c5e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260928194948096","summary":"Created a focused global ARC memory for straightening supported diagonal staircases without altering unrelated line fragments.","rationale":"The solved instance supports removing off-axis staircase cells and filling a straight run only where the pattern indicates a connection; repeated unsupported fills and changes to unrelated fragments failed.","expectedOutcome":"Future ARC solving can recognize this pattern while limiting edits to evidenced connections; validate that proposed fills are supported by the staircase and preserve unrelated straight segments.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_straighten_supported_diagonal_staircases","title":"ARC: Straighten supported diagonal staircases into aligned runs","content":"For sparse single-color ARC grids, consider whether a diagonal staircase is a detour connecting otherwise aligned horizontal or vertical segments. Apply only when the staircase and aligned pieces support the same straight run: replace the off-axis staircase cells with the supported continuous straight run. Remove isolated diagonal clutter only when the examples support doing so. Do not globally join unrelated collinear fragments, fill gaps without evidence, or erase clean horizontal/vertical pieces. Validate candidate changes against the demonstrated structure; unsupported broad fills and edits to unrelated fragments can fail.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The trajectory's successful solution supports this cautious rule, while multiple incorrect guesses demonstrate the risk of indiscriminately filling collinear gaps or altering unrelated fragments.","after":{"id":"arc_straighten_supported_diagonal_staircases","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: Straighten supported diagonal staircases into aligned runs","content":"For sparse single-color ARC grids, consider whether a diagonal staircase is a detour connecting otherwise aligned horizontal or vertical segments. Apply only when the staircase and aligned pieces support the same straight run: replace the off-axis staircase cells with the supported continuous straight run. Remove isolated diagonal clutter only when the examples support doing so. Do not globally join unrelated collinear fragments, fill gaps without evidence, or erase clean horizontal/vertical pieces. Validate candidate changes against the demonstrated structure; unsupported broad fills and edits to unrelated fragments can fail.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T19:49:58.802Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T19:49:58.802Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545240/attempts/f30eea74d21e478487daf9feda584c5e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260928222732315","summary":"Added a global ARC memory for filling separator-defined rectangular regions with nested color bands guided by diagonal markers.","rationale":"The solved task and three demonstration pairs support preserving separators and using marker colors along an informative diagonal to determine successive inward rectangular layers; the solved test also validates uniform fill for a single corner color and alternating layers from a background corner followed by a marker.","expectedOutcome":"Future ARC solving can recognize this region-and-marker structure, infer band colors and order from the aligned diagonal, preserve separator lines, and verify behavior against the input before applying the rule.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_separator_regions_diagonal_nested_color_bands","title":"ARC: Fill separator-defined rectangular regions with diagonal-guided nested color bands","content":"For ARC grids where separator lines divide the canvas into rectangular regions and colors placed along a diagonal provide fill cues, first verify the structure against the task examples. Preserve separator lines. Process each region independently: identify the informative corner from the aligned marker sequence, read the colors along its inward diagonal, and use that sequence for successive inward rectangular layers, repeating it only when supported by the examples. Do not assume a fixed global corner direction. Leave an unmarked region at its background. A region with a single informative corner color may fill uniformly; when the corner is the background and the next diagonal cell is a marker color, the demonstrated behavior is alternating background/marker layers. Validated test: in a 13×10 grid with color-7 separators, the unmarked upper-left region stayed color 4, the upper-right region with corner marker 9 became uniformly 9, and the lower region with diagonal colors 8 then 3 became nested layers 8, 3, 8. Treat these as demonstrated cases, not universal defaults; confirm marker alignment and layer behavior from available examples before transforming.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The user explicitly requested a reusable global memory, and the solved test plus three demonstrations provide evidence for this bounded transformation.","after":{"id":"arc_separator_regions_diagonal_nested_color_bands","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: Fill separator-defined rectangular regions with diagonal-guided nested color bands","content":"For ARC grids where separator lines divide the canvas into rectangular regions and colors placed along a diagonal provide fill cues, first verify the structure against the task examples. Preserve separator lines. Process each region independently: identify the informative corner from the aligned marker sequence, read the colors along its inward diagonal, and use that sequence for successive inward rectangular layers, repeating it only when supported by the examples. Do not assume a fixed global corner direction. Leave an unmarked region at its background. A region with a single informative corner color may fill uniformly; when the corner is the background and the next diagonal cell is a marker color, the demonstrated behavior is alternating background/marker layers. Validated test: in a 13×10 grid with color-7 separators, the unmarked upper-left region stayed color 4, the upper-right region with corner marker 9 became uniformly 9, and the lower region with diagonal colors 8 then 3 became nested layers 8, 3, 8. Treat these as demonstrated cases, not universal defaults; confirm marker alignment and layer behavior from available examples before transforming.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T22:27:37.955Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T22:27:37.955Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeage