{"id":"refine_20260929011921986","summary":"Created a global ARC memory for coloring shape slots from matching scattered sample objects.","rationale":"Two demonstrations and a correct held-out submission support identifying a template rectangle, matching its disconnected shape slots to monochrome sample objects up to rotation, and assigning colors by matching-shape frequency; orientation coverage helped resolve a demonstrated tie.","expectedOutcome":"Future solving can recognize this template-and-samples pattern, preserve the template background and dimensions, and validate shape matches and any tie-break before submitting.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_pattern_template_coloring_from_matching_objects","title":"ARC: Color pattern-template slots from matching objects (general, v1)","content":"For ARC grids where a rectangular region forms a template—uniform background with disconnected monochrome shape slots—and other regions contain scattered monochrome objects, first verify the layout and that slot shapes correspond to sample-object shapes. Match shapes allowing rotations; for each slot shape, use the most frequent color among matching sample objects. If frequencies tie, matching-orientation coverage may break the tie when supported by the template and demonstrations. Preserve the template background and slot geometry, recolor only the slots, and output the template rectangle. Do not apply without evidence that the regions encode a template and matching object samples.","metadata":{"scope":"global","evidence":"Two demonstrations and one correct held-out submission for task-9809adf1"},"reason":"Persists a validated, reusable ARC pattern-matching rule while keeping application conditional on the demonstrated structure.","after":{"id":"arc_pattern_template_coloring_from_matching_objects","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: Color pattern-template slots from matching objects (general, v1)","content":"For ARC grids where a rectangular region forms a template—uniform background with disconnected monochrome shape slots—and other regions contain scattered monochrome objects, first verify the layout and that slot shapes correspond to sample-object shapes. Match shapes allowing rotations; for each slot shape, use the most frequent color among matching sample objects. If frequencies tie, matching-orientation coverage may break the tie when supported by the template and demonstrations. Preserve the template background and slot geometry, recolor only the slots, and output the template rectangle. Do not apply without evidence that the regions encode a template and matching object samples.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global","evidence":"Two demonstrations and one correct held-out submission for task-9809adf1"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T01:19:27.093Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T01:19:27.093Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545240/attempts/f30eea74d21e478487daf9feda584c5e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929012132217","summary":"Added a global ARC memory for filling a blank fourth panel using a marker-directed separator between patterns in the other panels.","rationale":"The task was solved correctly using this rule, and the user explicitly requested that it be retained globally.","expectedOutcome":"Future matching four-panel tasks can apply the demonstrated region assignment while checking the panel layout and separator structure against examples.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_marker_directed_separator_panel_composition","title":"ARC: Compose a blank panel using a marker-directed separator","content":"For ARC grids arranged as four panels, consider this rule only when three panels contain a 5×5 separator-and-marker diagram plus two patterns, and the fourth panel is blank. In the diagram, a continuous 5-cell path acts as a partition boundary and an isolated marker identifies one side. Fill the blank panel with the second panel's pattern on the side containing the marker and the third panel's pattern on the opposite side. On separator cells, use the second panel's pattern when both patterns contribute. Preserve all other panels and verify the interpretation against the demonstrations before applying.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The rule was validated by a correct submission and is explicitly requested as a reusable cross-session ARC memory.","after":{"id":"arc_marker_directed_separator_panel_composition","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: Compose a blank panel using a marker-directed separator","content":"For ARC grids arranged as four panels, consider this rule only when three panels contain a 5×5 separator-and-marker diagram plus two patterns, and the fourth panel is blank. In the diagram, a continuous 5-cell path acts as a partition boundary and an isolated marker identifies one side. Fill the blank panel with the second panel's pattern on the side containing the marker and the third panel's pattern on the